module tools.multi_module_compile_fixture

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List }
import gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census { CompileDiagnosticCensusRow }

// THE ISOLATED MULTI-MODULE COMPILE FIXTURE. A caller authors a MANIFEST of `.dag` modules and
// an entry among them; the instrument compiles exactly those bytes through the v1 pipeline with
// no module index, no corpus roots, and no filesystem read, and returns what the compile decided.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS BESIDE THE SINGLE-MODULE CENSUS RATHER THAN INSIDE IT. `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`
// resolves its one synthetic module's imports against the live checkout, so the corpus is always
// in the pool and a SECOND module can never be authored. That makes every question whose subject
// is the relationship between two modules unaskable on it: a cross-module name collision, an
// import that must not bind, a spelling held by both a fixture declaration and a corpus one.
// Three consumers hit that wall independently before this instrument existed -- invalid fixtures
// relocated out of the corpus for want of it, an ambiguity arm measured and deleted for want of
// it, and DESIGN naming the missing multi-module compile fixture as the next-rung trigger for the
// acyclicity class, whose own receipt records that the single-module harness returns ParseError
// for the ACYCLIC control as well as the cyclic one and so could never reach the wall.
//
// IT IS AN INSTRUMENT AND NOT A GATE. Its outcome reports what the compile decided; nothing here
// judges acceptance, and no workflow invokes it. Callers judge.

// CORPUS ISOLATION IS BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY A FLAG, and that is the property the whole instrument
// rests on. The supplied manifest IS the source vector handed to the pipeline: there is no code
// path in the host realization that consults a module index, so a fixture module may reuse a corpus
// module's SPELLING and bind its own declaration. THE OPEN QUESTION THIS ANSWERS, recorded because
// upstream did not know the answer and asked for it either way: under whole-corpus floor
// preparation the required floor folds every witness through ONE prepared subject, so a name
// collision between a witness and the corpus is unconditional there. That preparation is a fact
// about how WITNESSES are compiled, not about this nested compile - the prepared authority is
// consulted by unrelated censuses and never by compile_to_resolved - so a fixture compiled through
// this instrument is isolated even when the caller itself was prepared whole-corpus. Control 5 of
// the witness measures exactly that rather than assuming it.

// PHASE-GRAINED STANDING IS THE POINT OF THE COPRODUCT. FixtureInstrumentRefused is the HARNESS's
// fault - a ragged manifest, a duplicate path, an entry naming no supplied module, a panic.
// FixtureCompileRefused is the SUBJECT's fault and carries the compiler's own judgment.
// FixtureCompileCompleted is the subject passing. Collapsing the first two hands a broken harness
// the compiler's message, which is the not-applicable-versus-malformed conflation DESIGN records as
// a recurring failure mode, applied to the instrument itself: one reason symbol over 'the input is
// wrong' and 'this instrument had nothing to say about it', which have opposite owners and opposite
// repairs. EXECUTION PROVENANCE IS STRUCTURAL AND NOT A FIELD ANYONE MUST REMEMBER TO READ:
// FixtureCompileCompleted is constructed at exactly one place in the host, after the emit returned
// and after the blocking population was counted, so an unreached or killed compile HAS NO SPELLING
// in which it renders as zero diagnostics. That is the positive-artifact rule moved into the type
// rather than asserted about it.

// THE TWO DIGESTS TRAVEL WITH THE RESULT so a receipt quoting this instrument names the subject it
// measured and the compiler that measured it. source_digest is a structural fnv1a64 over the entry
// spelling and every (path, content) pair in the order supplied - a reordering is a different
// subject and a whitespace edit is a different subject. compiler_digest is resolved_graph_cache
// transform_content_digest, the content hash of the running compiler binary, which is already the
// single authority every identity key in the seed consumes for compiler identity rather than a
// second executable read. Both are absent from the FixtureInstrumentRefused arm on purpose: that
// arm reports that no subject was formed, and stamping a digest on it would name a measurement that
// did not happen.

// SCOPE, stated so a receipt cannot claim coverage it does not have (DESIGN 4b: a class's rung is
// the MINIMUM across its in-scope paths). This is the v1 pipeline to the RUST render target over an
// authored multi-module subject. It observes NOTHING about the interpreter's disposition of the
// same program, nothing about other emission targets, and nothing about corpus-grain prevalence.
// Unlike the single-module census it does NOT arm with_type_ref_hit_ne_bind_measure: the census
// arms that bracket to sharpen masked type refs against a corpus pool this instrument does not
// have, so arming it here would be a knob with no subject. WHAT module_count MEANS: the number of
// modules the RESOLVE stage produced, read from the resolved graph - not the length of the supplied
// list. A control comparing it against the manifest is therefore a join between two different
// producers rather than measure() == measure().
//
// SIGNATURE SHAPE IS OBSERVABLE AS A STRUCTURED RUST PROJECTION OVER THE RESOLVED
// REGISTRY, not as contents-by-path and not as a read of emitted bytes. On
// FixtureCompileCompleted the field resolved_rust_functions carries List<ResolvedRustFnSignature>
// — per FnItem/FuncItem in item_registry: source owner_module + declaration_name and the
// ordered parameter name list derived from that registry row (emit_ident / service_var_name
// transforms applied). Witnesses assert over that list; raw text is not offered as an assertion
// surface. Discriminating RED: test.claim.resolved_rust_fn_projection_witness.
//
// THIS IS NOT AN EMITTED-ARTIFACT OBSERVATION. The projection runs on the resolved graph before
// emit_resolved_for_target; a defect solely inside emit_func_params / emit_func_def can leave
// witnesses green. The subject grain is the registry binding the emitter *will read*, not the
// bytes it writes. Next-rung trigger below covers climbing to a true emit join.

type FixtureSource {
  path: NonEmptyStr
  content: String
}

type MultiModuleCompileFixture {
  sources: List<FixtureSource>
  entry: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE STRUCTURED PROJECTION WITNESSES ASSERT OVER. Per resolved FnItem/FuncItem on each
// TypedModule.item_registry (NOT the bare-name-merged ResolvedGraph.item_registry): source
// identity (owning module + declaration name) and the PARAMETER NAME LIST taken from ItemInfo
// with the same per-arm name transforms emit_func_params applies today (emit_ident for authored
// and resource arms; service_var_name for services), concatenated authored → resource → service.
// The merged graph registry is bare-name keyed across the closure; publishing from it alone would
// drop colliding names and make lookup report Absent for a function that resolved — fail-open on
// every negative control if we only published the survivor. So the walk is per TypedModule and
// rows are keyed (owner_module, declaration_name). When the same bare Fn/Func name appears in two
// or more modules, those rows are OMITTED (lookup → ResolvedRustFnAbsent) — never
// FixtureInstrumentRefused, and never a fallback to the un-expanded per-module ItemInfo.
// expand_transitive_services writes only the merged registry, and emit_func_def looks up services
// by bare name on that same map; publishing the loser's un-expanded row would omit service_names
// while the emitter still binds the survivor's — a silent wrong answer. Present/absent consumers
// already treat Absent as false on both polarities, so omission is fail-closed. Non-colliding
// names overlay the expanded graph row when it still names this module under that bare name.

// The type name says Rust because compile_fixture is Rust-target only; a target-agnostic name
// over that reader would be the DESIGN section 3 meaning fork. The field name
// resolved_rust_functions / ResolvedRustFnSignature names the registry grain (and the Rust
// target); it does not claim a read of emitted file bytes — an `emitted_*` spelling over this
// pre-emit reader would be the meaning fork DESIGN §3 forbids.
//
// NAMES ONLY — PERMANENT CEILING, NO NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER. Parameter TYPES and return types are
// absent on purpose: asserting over emitted type spellings turns a control into a change detector
// for Rc<Vec<T>> renderings. Consequence: this projection cannot distinguish two parameters that
// differ only in type, and cannot see a type-only change that matters. That is a deliberate
// boundary of this carrier's contract, not a stall below a higher rung — there is no capability
// this instrument is waiting on that would lift it; a type-sensitive claim needs a different
// projection. Do not assert type-sensitivity over it.
//
// REGISTRY MIRROR, NOT EMIT JOIN — BELOW CEILING (ORDER AND MEMBERSHIP). DESIGN §3b middle
// value: deliberate divergence from single-authority emit binding, WITH A STATED REASON — not
// an unstated fork. The projection is a second walk over ItemInfo, not a consumption of
// emit_func_params' value. WHAT REFUSES IF emit_func_params (or emit_func_def's use of it) AND
// THIS PROJECTION DISAGREE — on order OR on who is present? Nothing. Concrete drift surface
// already visible: emit_func_params' resource arm folds `uses` nodes via resource_use_name_at;
// this walk reads ItemInfo.resource_names. A reorder, a dropped service_names arm inside emit,
// or resource-name divergence leaves every witness green while the artifact binds differently
// (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode subject_and_its_digest_as_independent_parameters).
//
// WHY THE DIVERGENCE IS THE RIGHT SHAPE FOR THIS INSTRUMENT (the §3b reason, not a manager
// citation). Consumers of compile_fixture need a membership surface over resolved fn bindings
// without coupling every fixture witness to emit_rust regeneration. The durable contract here is
// registry-grain lookup + resolved_rust_fn_has_parameter; that API is what must survive. The
// second walk is the temporary producer — §6's "will this artifact survive" test applies to the
// throwaway walk, which the next-rung trigger deletes, not to the carrier or its consumers.
// Joining emit on this same PR would turn an instrument lane into an emit_rust regen lane and
// couple it to #10688's live surface on the same files — two held PRs each waiting on the other.
// DESIGN §5: an in-diff row claiming approval does not authorize the debt; the reason above is
// what admits the middle value.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (DESIGN 4b(2) — the CAPABILITY, not an artifact): the projection is DERIVED
// from the same source emit_func_params reads (or from the emit result that source produces),
// rather than mirroring ItemInfo by a second walk, so neither order nor membership can
// desynchronise from what the Rust emitter binds into the artifact. WHY UNBUILT (reason, not
// trigger): that derivation lives in the emit_rust / emit_core_support seed surface; landing it
// here means regenerating those modules and crossing #10688's open edit surface on the same
// files. Until the trigger capability holds, this remains a registry projection. The honest rung
// (§4b(1) minimum across paths) is this order/membership stall, not the names-only permanent
// ceiling above it.
//
// THIS IS NOT A CONTENTS GREP SURFACE. Emitted file paths still travel on FixtureCompileCompleted
// for provenance; the projection is the only population a witness may ask about signature shape
// at this grain. A consumer that wants raw text is outside this instrument's contract — handing
// String would re-author the permanently-green substring trap this projection exists to make
// unwritable.
type ResolvedRustFnSignature {
  owner_module: String
  declaration_name: String
  ordered_parameter_names: List<String>
}

type ResolvedRustFnLookup
  = ResolvedRustFnFound { signature: ResolvedRustFnSignature }
  | ResolvedRustFnAbsent

type MultiModuleCompileFixtureOutcome
  = FixtureInstrumentRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | FixtureCompileRefused {
      module_count: Int
      diagnostics: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>
      source_digest: NonEmptyStr
      compiler_digest: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | FixtureCompileCompleted {
      module_count: Int
      emitted_files: List<String>
      resolved_rust_functions: List<ResolvedRustFnSignature>
      diagnostics: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>
      source_digest: NonEmptyStr
      compiler_digest: NonEmptyStr
    }

fn fixture_source(path: String, content: String) -> FixtureSource {
  FixtureSource { path: path, content: content }
}

fn compile_fixture(fixture: MultiModuleCompileFixture) -> MultiModuleCompileFixtureOutcome {
  compile_dag_multi_module_fixture(
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.path as String),
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.content),
    fixture.entry as String
  )
}

// PURE READS OVER ROWS, never over the outcome -- the same construction gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census
// adopted after an accessor answering [] on the not-runnable arm was found to be the fail-open it
// exists to prevent. The only way to obtain a diagnostic list here is to MATCH the coproduct, so
// reading zero diagnostics out of a refused instrument is structurally unwritable.
fn fixture_blocking_rows(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>) -> List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow> {
  rows |> filter(r => r.blocking)
}

fn fixture_rows_of_class(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>, wanted: String) -> List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow> {
  rows |> filter(r => (r.diagnostic_class as String) == wanted)
}

fn fixture_has_class(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>, wanted: String) -> Bool {
  count(fixture_rows_of_class(rows, wanted)) > 0
}

// PURE READS OVER THE PROJECTION LIST, never over the outcome. Looking up a function by source
// identity returns ResolvedRustFnAbsent when no row matches — never an empty parameter list wearing
// a found answer (DESIGN section 5: blank is not evidence of a nullary function). Consumers that
// assert a parameter is ABSENT must require ResolvedRustFnFound and then negate membership —
// mapping Absent to "lacks parameter" greens when the row was dropped entirely.
fn resolved_rust_fn_lookup(
  functions: List<ResolvedRustFnSignature>,
  owner_module: String,
  declaration_name: String
) -> ResolvedRustFnLookup {
  let matches = functions |> filter(f =>
    f.owner_module == owner_module && f.declaration_name == declaration_name
  )
  match matches |> first {
    Present { value: sig } => ResolvedRustFnFound { signature: sig }
    Absent => ResolvedRustFnAbsent
  }
}

// MEMBERSHIP, not position. Prefer this over indexing ordered_parameter_names unless the
// subject's claim is specifically about emit_func_params layout.
fn resolved_rust_fn_has_parameter(signature: ResolvedRustFnSignature, parameter_name: String) -> Bool {
  signature.ordered_parameter_names |> any(n => n == parameter_name)
}

// BUILDING A CLAIM SCOPE OVER THE SAME MANIFEST, which is a different question from compiling it.
//
// `compile_fixture` above stops at `compile_to_resolved`. The bare-name-ambiguity refusal lives
// in `claim_scope_for` -- the scope builder the required floor calls once per claim module, and
// the same call whose `ambiguous_reads` vector the floor's census lines are printed from. So a
// control written over `compile_fixture` cannot see that refusal at all, and the refusal's
// discriminating RED is not authorable in the CORPUS either: a module carrying a deliberately
// ambiguous read would refuse the whole floor rather than sit in it as a probe. DESIGN section 4b
// answers exactly that shape -- a state unrepresentable in the accepted corpus may still be
// representable as source handed to the compiler by a fixture, and declining to enrol it there is
// specification-without-execution.
//
// ONE DETECTOR, NOT TWO. The host builtin behind this calls `claim_scope_for_with_memos`, the
// same function the floor's own entry point calls. It does not re-derive the ambiguity
// population, so the refusal a control observes IS the refusal the corpus floor raises, from one
// computation. A second copy here -- even a faithful one -- would be the second authority the
// census exists to prevent, and would be free to drift from it silently. What differs from the
// floor's call is the memo arguments, which is a caching decision rather than a semantic one.
//
// THE ENTRY IS A MODULE PATH, not a file path, because that is what `claim_scope_for` is keyed on.
type ClaimScopeFixtureOutcome
  = ClaimScopeInstrumentRefused { cause: String }
  | ClaimScopeCompileRefused { blocking_count: Int }
  | ClaimScopeRefused { cause: String }
  | ClaimScopeAccepted { module_count: Int }

fn claim_scope_fixture(fixture: MultiModuleCompileFixture, entry_module_path: String) -> ClaimScopeFixtureOutcome {
  claim_scope_dag_multi_module_fixture(
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.path as String),
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.content),
    entry_module_path
  )
}

// AND WHICH REFUSAL IT WAS -- the only refusal reader this instrument offers, deliberately.
//
// A reader that answered "did the scope builder refuse" would say nothing about WHY, and a probe
// satisfied by ANY refusal goes green the day an unrelated one starts firing first, and would
// then be cited as coverage for a cause it never observed. One was written here and deleted
// rather than shipped beside this: two readers, one of them strictly weaker, is an invitation to
// reach for the weaker.
//
// So this reader matches the arm AND requires the cause to name the expected refusal: a red has
// to be the red the caller meant.
//
// Substring scan via the ambient split, the idiom the corpus already uses for this: a marker is
// contained when splitting on it yields more than one piece.
fn claim_scope_refusal_names(o: ClaimScopeFixtureOutcome, cause_marker: String) -> Bool {
  match o {
    ClaimScopeRefused { cause: c } => (split(s: c, delimiter: cause_marker) |> count) > 1
    ClaimScopeAccepted { module_count: n } => false
    ClaimScopeCompileRefused { blocking_count: n } => false
    ClaimScopeInstrumentRefused { cause: c } => false
  }
}

fn claim_scope_was_accepted(o: ClaimScopeFixtureOutcome) -> Bool {
  match o {
    ClaimScopeAccepted { module_count: n } => true
    ClaimScopeRefused { cause: c } => false
    ClaimScopeCompileRefused { blocking_count: n } => false
    ClaimScopeInstrumentRefused { cause: c } => false
  }
}
